<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050#"
     xml:base="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050"
     xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
     xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:bibo="http://purl.org/ontology/bibo/"
     xmlns:foaf="http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
     xmlns:vann="http://purl.org/vocab/vann/"
     xmlns:terms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/"
     xmlns:schema="https://schema.org/">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050">
        <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050/1.0.0"/>
        <terms:abstract xml:lang="en">An ontology design pattern for the terminology of control technology, following DIN IEC 60050-351:2014, the International Electrotechnical Vocabulary. It models a control as a chain of action paths and actions, which is what allows open-loop and closed-loop control to be told apart by structure rather than by name: a closed-loop control requires a closed action, not merely a closed path. Around that it holds the variable quantities of a control loop, the items under consideration with their functional and physical units, the normative taxonomy of control functions, and the characteristics a device carries with their values and units. Seventy-nine classes drawn from 86 of the standard&apos;s 409 entries, with the selection documented entry by entry.</terms:abstract>
        <terms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-18</terms:created>
        <terms:creator>Marvin Schieseck</terms:creator>
        <terms:hasVersion rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050"/>
        <terms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
        <terms:modified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-09-02</terms:modified>
        <terms:publisher>Helmut-Schmidt-Universität / Universität der Bundeswehr Hamburg</terms:publisher>
        <terms:rights xml:lang="en">This research is funded by dtec.bw – Digitalization and Technology Research Center of the Bundeswehr. dtec.bw is funded by the European Union – NextGenerationEU</terms:rights>
        <terms:source rdf:resource="https://www.dinmedia.de/de/norm/din-iec-60050-351/208013542"/>
        <terms:subject xml:lang="en">Automation</terms:subject>
        <terms:subject xml:lang="en">Closed-loop Control</terms:subject>
        <terms:subject xml:lang="en">Control Technology</terms:subject>
        <terms:subject xml:lang="en">IEC 60050-351</terms:subject>
        <terms:subject xml:lang="en">Ontology Design Pattern</terms:subject>
        <terms:title xml:lang="en">IEC 60050-351 Ontology Design Pattern</terms:title>
        <bibo:status xml:lang="en">Draft</bibo:status>
        <vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>iec60050</vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>
        <vann:preferredNamespaceUri>https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iec60050#</vann:preferredNamespaceUri>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Normative basis: DIN IEC 60050-351:2014, identical to IEC 60050-351:2013.

The standard holds 409 entries and this pattern takes up 86 of them. REFERENCE.md lists every entry with the reason it is taken or left out, so the selection is checkable rather than asserted. Roughly 150 entries are the mathematical apparatus of control theory, such as transfer functions and Nyquist plots, which an engineering model names but never instantiates.

Three readings of the standard are easy to get wrong and are therefore stated here. An actuator, 351-49-07, is the signal side driving the final controlling element, not the element acting on the flow. The final controlling element, 351-49-08, belongs to the controlled system, which this pattern records as a relation so that another pattern may hold it as a resource without contradiction. And the standard defines no sensor: it says sensing element, 351-56-26, and grades further by accuracy and signal standardisation.

Terms the standard does not define, and which have to come from elsewhere: availability and reliability from IEC 60050-192, accuracy and resolution from IEC 60050-300, safety integrity from IEC 61508, and everything concerning data quality from ISO/IEC 22989.</rdfs:comment>
        <owl:versionInfo>1.0.0</owl:versionInfo>
        <foaf:fundedBy rdf:resource="https://dtecbw.de/"/>
        <schema:codeRepository rdf:resource="https://github.com/schiesem/aias"/>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


